{
  "requirement": "Repository validation MUST continue after a failed check and report every failed\ncheck it finds in one run, each as a {term}`requirements.ci.validation_error`.\nThis is a convention for every validation error; the requirement for each check\nstates which conditions that check reports. A file that cannot be parsed, such as\na malformed `habeas.toml`, `book.toml`, or evidence file, is one failed check:\nits error MAY report only the first parse or decoding problem in that file, and\nchecks that do not depend on its contents MUST still run.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: `requirements.ci.errors` is met. The implementation continues after failures, and three tests cover the scenarios.**\n\n**Implementation**\n\n`inspect_repository` and `inspect_repository_with_progress` in `src/evidence.rs` carry `{impl}` markers for the requirement. `inspect_repository` is a thin wrapper around the progress variant, and `validate_repository` calls it. The orchestration works as the requirement states:\n\n- **Accumulation, not early exit.** Every phase appends to a shared `errors: Vec<String>`. The phases are workspace config load, `book_source_dir`, `collect_definitions`, evidence-file validation, missing-evidence coverage, reference collection and validation, and Rust item coverage. None of them returns early on a failed check. The final report carries all collected errors.\n- **Malformed `habeas.toml`.** `load_workspace_evidence` failing is recorded with `errors.push(error.to_string())`, and inspection carries on.\n- **Malformed `book.toml`.** `book_source_dir` records the specific error and falls back to `md`. Definition collection, evidence validation and reference scanning therefore still run.\n- **Malformed evidence file.** `validate_evidence_file` returns after the first read, JSON or object-shape error, which the requirement allows (\"MAY report only the first parse or decoding problem\"). The file is still counted as present before validation, so no duplicate \"missing\" error is added. Every other evidence file, every definition and every source reference is still checked.\n- **I/O failures.** Directory listing, entry, chapter-read and reference-source failures each push a located error and `continue`.\n- **Error shape.** Messages begin with the file or `file:line` and name the failed check, which matches `validation_error`. `DefinitionKind::noun` in `src/lib.rs` supplies the wording and is marked.\n\nI did not count the main.rs pre-load as a gap. In `src/main.rs`, `main` loads workspace evidence config with `?` before the status, info, author and add-input commands. That is command-level configuration loading, governed by `requirements.configuration.loading`. It is not the repository-validation pipeline this requirement covers.\n\n**Tests**\n\nAll three tests carry `{test}` markers for the requirement:\n\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` (src/evidence.rs, about line 1544) creates many simultaneous failures in one run. They include duplicate and empty requirements, a stale requirement text, a false determination, several malformed input kinds, a malformed evidence file and an orphan file. It asserts that all of these appear in a single report.\n- `configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks` (about line 1834) covers three cases:\n  - Several unresolved terms are all reported, while the other record's checks still run.\n  - An invalid `habeas.toml` and an invalid `book.toml` are each reported.\n  - After those two failures, a malformed evidence file and an unknown source reference are still reported. This is the \"checks that do not depend on its contents MUST still run\" case.\n- `inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase` (about line 1340) injects a broken evidence file, an unparsable Rust source and an uncited function. It asserts that all of those errors are reported alongside the valid record, and that discovery errors stay separate.\n\n**Minor observation, not a gap**\n\nA malformed evidence file for a real definition can also trigger a separate \"no input covers …\" reference-coverage error. That check depends on the file's contents, so reporting it does not violate the requirement.",
